.TH SNQUOTA 1 "March 2020" "Xsan File System"
.SH NAME
snquota \- Xsan Quota Configuration Utility
.SH SYNOPSIS
.na
.nh
.B snquota
.RI {\fB-F\fP \ volume_name |\fB-P\fP \ path }
.I action
.RI [ options ]
.ad
.hy
.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBsnquota\fR command manipulates the quota system in the
\fIXsan volume\fR.
.PP
The quota system provides a means for limiting the amount of disk storage
consumed on a per user or per group basis across an entire file system or 
within a designated directory hierarchy.
Quota limits apply to the space consumed by disk-block allocations for a user 
or group, which is not equal to the sum of their file sizes.  
Disk-block allocations can be less than the file size if the file is sparse,
or more if the file system has allocated extra sequential blocks for the
efficiency of anticipated future writes.
.PP
There are three types of quotas:
user quotas, group quotas, and directory quotas.
User and group quotas limit the number of volume blocks that can be 
allocated
by the user or group on which the limit is placed.  When quotas are
on, the total allocated volume space of all users and groups that own
files in the volume are automatically kept.
.PP
Directory quotas are a little different.  The system does not
automatically keep track of the usage for each directory.  The
\fBsnquota\fR command allows directories to be turned into the root of
a \fBDirectory Quota Name Space (DQNS)\fR.  Then, the number and size
of all files in the directory and all its subdirectories are tracked
and (optionally) limited.
.PP
For all quota types, limits and usage values only apply to regular
files, not directories, symlinks, or special device files
.PP
Each quota entity has two limits associated with it.  These are the
hard limit and the soft limit.
.PP
The hard limit is the absolute limit which volume space usage should 
not exceed.  Any time the total allocated space is at or over the hard limit,
all further allocations or write requests by the offending user or group 
will be denied.
.PP
The soft limit is a lesser limit.
When the user exceeds this limit (but not the
hard limit), allocations are still permitted for a while, but a
warning message will be written to the logs.  (View the messages with
\fBConsole.app\fR or the \fBdmesg\fR command.)
When the soft limit has been overrun for longer than the grace period,
the soft limit becomes a hard limit and any further allocations or
write requests are denied.  When the usage again falls below the soft
limit, allocation requests will again be serviced.
.PP
Previous versions of \fBsnquota\fR required all three of the limit
values (hard, soft, and grace) to be set or all three to be unset.
This version relaxes that somewhat.  It is now possible to specify
just a hard limit without a soft and grace limit.  It's also possible
to set a soft limit and grace period without a hard limit.  It's also
possible to specify a soft limit without any grace period (such a
limit warns periodically, but never prevents allocation).  It's still
not possible to specify a grace period without a soft limit. Although
all three values (\fB-h\fR, \fB-s\fR, \fB-t\fR) still need to be on
the command line, you can set the limits that should not be
enforced to zero.
.PP
For performance reasons related to the distributed nature of Xsan,
quota overruns are not only possible but likely.  The overrun size
depends upon a number of factors including the size of the 
allocation request(s) at the time of the quota overrun.
.PP
Some versions of \fBsnquota\fR support setting default limits for the
various types of quotas.  For example, you can set a default hard
limit, soft limit, and grace period for all users.  After doing that,
any new user created will inherit those limits.  Note that setting
default limits will not change the limits of existing quota entities.
Use the \fB-0\fR option and either "\fB-u default\fR", "\fB-g
default\fR", "\fB-d default\fR", or "\fB-fd default\fR".  See the
examples section.
.PP
When working with Directory Quotas, the specified volume must
be mounted on the node running \fBsnquota\fR.
.PP
Limits are not enforced against super-user accounts.
.SH DIRECTORY QUOTA NAME SPACES
DQNSs are created by either of two actions, \fB-C\fR or \fB-M\fR.
They have different performance trade-offs and which one to use
depends on the situation at hand.
.PP
The \fB-C\fR action creates a DQNS whose usage values (the amount of
disk space and the number of files) are already initialized to the
correct value.  In order to initialize them, \fBsnquota\fR must walk
the directory tree under the root of the DQNS and tally up how much
disk space is used.  For big directory trees, this process can take a
long time.  Any modifications to the files and directories in the DQNS
will be stalled until this walk is complete.
.PP
The \fB-M\fR action quickly creates a DQNS whose usage values are
zero.  As files are created in the DQNS, the usage value will
increase, but will never count the files that were present in the
directory when it was created.  In order to initialize the DQNS
so the values are correct, the quota database must be rebuild using
the \fB-R\fR action.  A rebuild runs much faster than a file-tree walk
on a per-inode basis, but it must look at \fBall\fR of the inodes in
the volume.  When the rebuild is running, modifications to
the volume will be stalled until the rebuild is complete.
.PP
So, when creating a DQNS that is believed to contain only a small
percentage of the inodes in the volume, use \fB-C\fR.  When
creating a DQNS (or many DQNSs) that use a large percentage of the
files, use \fB-M\fR.
.PP
A typical situation where \fB-M\fR would be useful is converting an
existing volume to use directory quotas.  First every
directory which needs to be a DQNS root is marked with a call to
"snquota \fB-M\fR".  Then, all of the DQNSs are initialized with
one call to "snquota \fB-R\fR".
.PP
When in doubt, use \fB-C\fR.
.PP
Nesting of DQNSs is not allowed.  This means that a DQNS may not be a
subdirectory of another DQNS.
.PP
Directories can not be renamed across DQNS boundaries.  Also, all
hard links to an inode must be within the same DQNS.  Attempts to
rename directories/files or create hard links that would violate this
rule will result in a EXDEV being returned.
.PP
If a directory tree contains inodes with hard links outside of the
tree, an attempt to convert the tree into a DQNS via the \fB-C\fR
action on the tree will result in an error.  An attempt to convert the
tree into a DQNS via the \fB-M\fR and \fB-R\fR actions will result in
an error during the \fB-R\fR action.
.SH QUOTAS IN MIXED OS ENVIRONMENTS
.PP
The user and groups names specified in \fB-u\fR and \fB-g\fR represent
underlying identifiers that are determined by the OS type of the MDC.
.PP
On a Linux or Xsan MDC, those
identifiers are the classic UNIX User IDentifier (UID) and Group
IDentifier (GID).  When a UNIX client (Linux, MacOS, Solaris, etc)
creates a file, it passes the user's UID and GID to the MDC.  Those
IDs are attached to the file and are used by the quota subsystem.
When a Windows client creates a file, it passes a UID and GID it gets
from one of three places:
.PP
.IP 1.
If the Active Directory entry for the user has UNIX IDs associated
with it, those are used.  The behavior at this point is just like UNIX
client.  The administrator can set the IDs for a user via the AD
configuration tool under the "UNIX Attributes" tab.  This tab is part
of the "Identity Management for UNIX" subsystem.
.IP 2.
If the user doesn't have UNIX attributes, then the user and group
"nobody" IDs from the file system configuration file are used.
.IP 3.
If the process's SID is a special "Root SID", the UID/GID passed will
be 0/0 (i.e. root).  The "root SID" is S-1-5-18.
.PP
The Windows client can associate a NTSD with a file, but it's ignored
by the quota subsystem. (It's only used for access control by the
client at that point.)
.PP
On a Windows MDC, the favored identifiers are user and group SIDs
derived from the NTSD which owns the file.  If there is no NTSD
associated with the file, the UID/GID values associated with the inode
are used.  So, when a Windows client creates a file, it passes in a
NTSD.  That NTSD broken into SIDs and used as file's owner identifiers
as far as the quota subsystem is concerned.  When a UNIX client
creates a file, it passes the usual UID/GID pair, not a NTSD.  This is
used by the quota system.  If that file is accessed from a on Windows
client, it gets assigned an NTSD.  At that point the quota will be
wrong.  Subsequent allocations of that file will be charged to the SD
and not the UID/GID.
.PP
So, the preferred method of running quotas with a mixture of UNIX and
Windows clients is to run with a Linux MDC with UNIX user/group
mappings for Active Directory users.  That way, a user who logs into
clients of either OS will have a single quota (which will be based on
the UID).
.PP
Another option is to just use Directory Quotas.  They are much more
straight-forward to share between OS types.
.SH UNITS
Usage and Limits are printed in a human-readable form, suffixed with
"K", "M", "G", "T","P", or "E" for kibibytes, mebibyte, gibibyte,
tebibyte, pebibytes, or exbibytes, respectively.  These are base-2 values
(i.e. 1K = 1024).  A value without a suffix is in bytes.
.PP
File count values are also printed with these suffixes, but they are
base-10 values (i.e. 1K = 1000).
.PP
Time values are printed with the suffixes "m", "h", "d", "w", "M" and "y" for
minutes, hours, days, weeks, months, and years, respectively.
.PP
If the \fB-e\fR option is used, the suffixes are disabled and exact
values are printed. Time units are in minutes.
.PP
These suffixes can also be used when specifying limits with the \fB-h\fR,
\fB-s\fR, and \fB-t\fR options.  Decimal values may be used (e.g -h
1.5g).
.PP
For the most part, the case of the suffix doesn't matter.  The only
exception to that is the time suffixes "m" and "M", meaning "minutes"
and "months", respectively.
.SH FILE SYSTEM SPECIFICATION
.IP "\fB-F\fP \fIVolumeName\fP"
Specify VolumeName as the volume to manipulate.
VolumeName may be qualified with cluster and administrative
domain, if other than the default cluster in which the command is
being run. The syntax is VolumeName@<cluster>[/<addom>].
.IP "\fB-P\fP \fIPath\fP"
Specify the volume containing \fIPath\fP as the
volume to manipulate.
.SH ACTIONS
.IP \fB-C\fR
This action creates an initialized DQNS on the directory specified by
the \fB-d\fR argument.  After this command is run, disk space usage
and file counts will be tracked in the directory and all its
subdirectories.  Later, limits may be set on the DQNS using the
\fB-S\fR action.  Note that since this operation creates \fIand
initializes\fR the DQNS, the directory tree contained by the new DQNS
will be walked to total up the current usage values.  This may take
some time.  Modifications to the files and directories in the DQNS
will be stalled until this walk is complete.
.IP \fB-D\fR
This action destroys the DQNS specified by the \fB-d\fR argument.
Disk space and file count usage values will no longer be tracked.
Limits will no longer be enforced.  Note that this does not modify or
destroy the files and directories in the DQNS in any way.
.IP \fB-G\fR
This action returns the quota limits and values for the user, group,
or directory specified by the \fB-u\fR, \fB-g\fR, or \fB-d\fR option,
respectively.
.IP \fB-L\fR
This action lists the current quota limits and values for all user,
group, and directory quotas.
.IP \fB-M\fR
This action creates (marks) an uninitialized DQNS on the directory
specified by the \fB-d\fR argument.  After this command is run, disk
space usage and file counts will be tracked in the directory and all
its subdirectories.  Later, limits may be set on the DQNS using the
\fB-S\fR option.  Note that since this operation creates \fI(but does
not initialize)\fR the DQNS, the usage values for the DQNS will start
out at zero.  The user should later use the \fB-R\fR action to
initialize the usage values.  See the \fBDIRECTORY QUOTA NAME
SPACES\fR section above for a discussion on when to use \fB-M\fR and
when to use \fB-C\fR.  When in doubt, don't use this action.  Use
\fB-C\fR instead.
.IP \fB-R\fR
This action rebuilds the quota database.  It is most useful when used
after \fBsnquota\fR has been used a number of times with the \fB-M\fR
action.  See the \fBDIRECTORY QUOTA NAME SPACES\fR section above.
Note that this action can take a long time.  The volume will
be unresponsive during this time.  The action cannot be canceled after
it is started.  A prompt will be displayed confirming the intent to
run the action unless the \fB-Y\fR option is specified. A rebuild
preserves limits and DQNSs.
.IP \fB-S\fR
This action sets the quota limits for the user, group, or directory
specified by the \fB-u\fR, \fB-g\fR, or \fB-d\fR option,
respectively.  The limits must be specified by the \fB-h\fR,
\fB-s\fR, and \fB-t\fR, options.  All three must be present, but
some/all may be zero.
.IP \fB-X\fR
This action generates quota reports for all users and groups.  There
are three files placed in \fI/Library/Logs/Xsan/data/\fP<volume_name>:
    1. quota_report.txt - a "pretty" text file report.
    2. quota_report.csv - a comma delimited report suitable
       for Excel spreadsheets.  Fields that can have commas,
       newlines, and other strange characters are escaped with
       double quotes.  This is the case with DQNSs path fields.
    3. quota_regen.in - a list of snquota commands that can be
       used to set up an identical quota database on another
       \fIXsan volume\fR. Passing this file to
       the shell executes it as a script.
.IP \fB-Z\fR
This action completely destroys all quota configuration
and then rebuilds the quota database as in the \fB-R\fR
option above. Unlike the  \fB-R\fR action,  \fB-Z\fR clears the limits and
directory quota name spaces.
It is recommended that the quota_regen.in file be generated using the
\fB-X\fR option to save the quota configuration prior to using
\fB-Z\fR.
.SH OPTIONS
.IP \fB-0\fR
List, read, and set default limits.  When specified with the \fB-L\fR
action, the default limits are included in the list (if they are
non-zero).  When specified with the \fB-G\fR or \fB-S\fR actions, the
default limits for the specified type of quota are read or written.
When using \fB-0\fR, the argument for \fB-u\fR, \fB-g\fR, \fB-d\fR, or
\fB-fd\fR should be "default".
.IP \fB-a\fR
When this option is used, directory quota paths printed by the
\fB-L\fR and \fB-G\fR options will be absolute paths.  Paths supplied
to the \fB-d\fR option are also absolute paths (or relative to the
CWD).  When this option is absent, all paths are relative to the root
of the specified volume.
.IP "\fB-d\fP \fIDirectory\fP"
This option specifies a DQNS on a \fIXsan volume\fR
to be used with the \fB-C\fR, \fB-D\fR, \fB-G\fR, \fB-M\fR, or
\fB-S\fR options.  The directory supplied is the root directory of the
DQNS.  The directory path is relative to the root of the specified
volume, unless the \fB-a\fR option is used.
.IP \fB-e\fR
When used with the \fB-G\fR or \fB-L\fR actions, numbers will be
printed as \fIexact\fR values.  Usage and Limits which represent disk
space are printed in bytes.  Times are printed in minutes.  For
example, with this option, a one megabyte hard limit will be printed
as "1048576", not "1M".  A one day grace period will be printed as
"1440", not "1d".
.IP \fB-f\fR
The \fB-f\fR option is only useful with the \fB-G\fR and \fB-S\fR
actions and the \fB-d\fR option.  When the \fB-f\fR option is present,
limits and values represent the number of regular files contained in
the DQNS.  If the \fB-f\fR option is not present, limits and values
represent the disk space contained in the DQNS.
.IP "\fB-g\fP \fIGroupName\fP"
This option specifies the name of a group to get or set with the
\fB-G\fR or \fB-S\fR action.  The group name may also be of the form
"G:id", where "id" is a number that represents a group's GID.
.IP "\fB-H\fP \fIHostName\fP"
Use a hostname in a Xsan cluster that is different from the
cluster the command is being run on.  This option in rarely needed.
.IP "\fB-h\fP \fIHardLimit\fP"
This option specifies a hard limit to set when used with the \fB-S\fR
action.  See the \fBUNITS\fR section above.  The maximum hard limit
value is 16 exbibytes - 1 fsBlockSize (the file system configured block
size.) If the fsBlockSize is 4096, the maximum hard limit value is
18,446,744,073,709,547,520 bytes.  (Note:
commas were added for readability; commas are not allowed in values.)
.IP \fB-h\fR
This option causes \fBsnquota\fR to print a friendly help message and
exit.  It only works when used by itself.  If there are other options
present, it is assumed that a hard limit is being specified.
.IP "\fB-o\fP {\fBtext\fP|\fBxml\fP|\fBjson\fP}"
Print output in \fBtext\fR, \fBxml\fR, or \fBjson\fR.
The default is \fBtext\fR.
.IP "\fB-s\fP \fISoftLimit\fP"
This option specifies a soft limit to set when used with the \fB-S\fR
action.  See the \fBUNITS\fR section above. The maximum soft limit
value is 16 exbibytes - 1 fsBlockSize (the file system configured block
size.) If the fsBlockSize is 4096, the maximum soft limit value is
18,446,744,073,709,547,520 bytes.  (Note:
commas were added for readability; commas are not allowed in values.)
.IP "\fB-t\fP \fIGracePeriod\fP"
This option specifies a grace period to set when used with the
\fB-S\fR action.  See the \fBUNITS\fR section above.
.IP "\fB-u\fP \fIUserName\fP"
This option specifies the name of a user to get or set with the
\fB-G\fR or \fB-S\fR action.  The user name may also be of the form
"U:id", where "id" is a number that represents a user's UID.
.IP \fB-Y\fR
When used with the \fB-R\fR or \fB-Z\fR actions, this option prevents
\fBsnquota\fR from asking for confirmation.
.IP \fB-z\fR
This option is the same as specifying "-h 0 -s 0 -t 0".  It's only
useful with the \fB-S\fR action.
.SH EXIT VALUES
\fBsnquota\fR will return 0 on success and non-zero on failure.
.SH EXAMPLES
List all the quota limits and values on a volume named
"data".
.Cs
snquota -F data -L
.Ce
.PP
List all the quota limits and values on a volume named
"data", but in cluster "cluster1".
.Cs
snquota -F data@cluster1 -L
.Ce
.PP
Specify a hard limit of ten gigabytes, a soft limit of nine gigabytes,
and a grace period of one week on user "lisa" in a volume
named "data".
.Cs
snquota -F data -S -u lisa -h 10g -s 9g -t 1w
.Ce
.PP
Turn off quota limits for user "lisa" in a volume named "data".
.Cs
snquota -F data -S -u lisa -z
.Ce
.PP
Get the quota values for a group named "simpsons" on a volume
mounted on "/stornext/data".
.Cs
snquota -P /stornext/data -G -g simpsons
.Ce
.PP
Create a \fBDQNS\fR on the directory
"/lisa/saxophone_music" in a volume mounted on "/stornext/data".
.Cs
snquota -P /stornext/data -C -d /lisa/saxophone_music
.Ce
.PP
Specify a hard limit of one gigabyte, a soft limit of nine hundred
megabytes, and a grace period of one day on pre-existing DQNS
"/lisa/saxophone_music" in a volume named "data".
.Cs
snquota -F data -S -d /lisa/saxophone_music -h 1g -s 900m -t 1d
.Ce
.PP
Create a number of DQNSs using \fB-M\fR and \fB-R\fR.  This is faster
than using \fB-C\fR if these directories take up most of the space in
the volume.
.Cs
snquota -F data -M -d /bart/comics
snquota -F data -M -d /bart/pranks
snquota -F data -M -d /bart/itchy_and_scratchy
snquota -F data -R
.Ce
.PP
Create the same DQNSs using \fB-C\fR.  This is faster than using
\fB-M\fR and \fB-R\fR if the directories are small.
.Cs
snquota -F data -C -d /bart/comics
snquota -F data -C -d /bart/pranks
snquota -F data -C -d /bart/itchy_and_scratchy
.Ce
.PP
Specify a hard limit of one thousand files, a soft limit of nine
hundred files, and a grace period of one week on pre-existing DQNS
"/bart/pranks" in a volume named "data".
.Cs
snquota -F data -S -d /bart/pranks -f -h 1k -s 900 -t 1w
.Ce
.PP
Set default hard limits limits for all newly created DQNSs.  The first
line set defaults for disk space and the second line sets defaults for
files.
.Cs
snquota -F data -S -0d default -h 1t -s 0 -t 0
snquota -F data -S -0fd default -h 1m -s 0 -t 0
.Ce
